// block/psu-01/backbone — the un-isolated bus interface: the port on two RJ45 jacks, a CAN FD
// transceiver the MCU can silence, and the RS-485 transceiver that puts the DCC sync on the pair.
// Ported from arc's `block::psu_01::backbone`.
//
// Every other node crosses the backbone through `arc_link`'s barrier — two isolators and a
// floating converter. This board cannot, and the reason is structural: psu-01 *sources* VLINK, so
// its ground and the backbone's ground are one node by construction, and an isolator here would
// have its two sides shorted by the power path beside it. This is the one board where the honest
// design is bare transceivers referencing the rail's own return.
//
// What the port is testing:
//
// - **A block re-exports a part's typed port line by line.** The MCU's CAN link lands on this
//   block's `fdcan`, which is the transceiver's `can` seen through two `circuit`s, so the board
//   answers the contract on the block rather than reaching into it.
// - **A requirement on a capability.** The DCC waveform must come off a DMA-fed counter — the
//   edges *are* the protocol — so `dcc-txd` is a `pwm` port that `require`s `signal.dma`, and a
//   pin that cannot reach such a channel is a finding rather than a firmware surprise.
// - **Two bodies are one port.** The jacks are tied conductor for conductor here, so a sheet
//   cannot wire one and not the other; the conductors are named once, at the port.

use "@stackup/passives"
use "@stackup/bus/can/tcan334g"
use "@stackup/bus/rs485/st3485eb"
use "../../shared/connectors.kdl"

block backbone {
    // The 3.3 V both transceivers run off — one domain for logic and bus sides alike, because this
    // is the un-isolated node.
    port logic type=power
    // The backbone rail, as a *demand*: this node makes it, on the rail sheet, and this sheet's job
    // is only to put it on the port.
    port link type=power

    // The MCU's end of the CAN logic link.
    port fdcan type=can
    // The transceiver's mode pins, vended rather than strapped: firmware holds the part quiet
    // until the rail is up, so the bus never sees a node half awake. Their internal pulldowns mean
    // an undriven part is simply on.
    port stb type=output
    port shdn type=output

    // The DCC driver's three lines. The waveform in, for a DMA-fed timer channel — this node is
    // the sync generator, and the edges are the protocol; the enable, active high and pulled down
    // here; and the pair read back, always on, so firmware can compare what it sent with what the
    // bus carries.
    port dcc-txd type=pwm {
        require signal.dma
    }
    port dcc-de type=output
    port dcc-rxd

    // The CAN FD transceiver — bare, where every other node is isolated. Its mode pins are real
    // ports; nothing is strapped.
    place tcan334g can vcc=logic designator=CAN
    circuit fdcan.tx can.can.tx
    circuit fdcan.rx can.can.rx
    circuit stb can.stb
    circuit shdn can.shdn

    // The DCC driver. What is wired here is what the node *is*: ~RE low keeps the receiver on
    // regardless, so the MCU reads back the pair it is driving and a contended or dead bus is a
    // comparison rather than a guess; and DE carries a pulldown, so an MCU that has let go of the
    // line — unprogrammed, in reset, mid-boot — leaves the pair idle, exactly as the rail sheet's
    // switch leaves the rail dark. The ST3485EB rather than the MAX3485 it is pin-for-pin with,
    // for the pair it drives: A/B meet a hot-plugged patch lead in a cabinet, and this part's bus
    // pins stand ±15 kV IEC air discharge where the plain MAX3485's stand 2 kV HBM.
    place st3485eb dcc vcc=logic designator=DCC
    circuit dcc.re logic.gnd
    place pull-down de-pulldown node=dcc.de rail=logic value="10kΩ" \
        note="DE pulldown — the pair idles when the MCU lets go"
    circuit dcc-txd dcc.di
    circuit dcc-de dcc.de
    circuit dcc-rxd dcc.ro

    // The port: two 8P8C jacks in parallel, which is one port on two bodies — a lead arrives on
    // either and a second lead carries the same six conductors on to the next node, so a run of
    // nodes can be a chain instead of six drops back to a hub. Still not a hub, and it terminates
    // nothing: no 120 Ω on either pair, because the wiring centre terminates the buses, a node
    // that carried its own would double-load the star once per node plugged in, and a pass-through
    // can now sit mid-trunk, where a terminator is the classic CAN installation fault. LNK1/LNK2
    // on the silk: a cable goes in either, so the legend is the bus's name rather than a
    // connector number.
    scope port {
        place backbone-jack a reference=LNK \
            note="backbone jack — the rail on pairs 1/2 and 3/6, CAN on 4/5, DCC on 7/8; in parallel with the other, so a run passes through"
        place backbone-jack b reference=LNK \
            note="the same port on a second body — a lead carries the run on to the next node"
        circuit a.VLINK b.VLINK link.rail
        circuit a.GND b.GND link.gnd
        circuit a.CANH b.CANH can.CANH name="CANH"
        circuit a.CANL b.CANL can.CANL name="CANL"
        circuit a.DCC_A b.DCC_A dcc.A name="DCC_A"
        circuit a.DCC_B b.DCC_B dcc.B name="DCC_B"
    }
}
